{"archetypes":[{"name":"teardown-flow","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff adds a Step 18b wrapper and rewires final-summary emission, sentinels, token refresh, and teardown sequencing.","prompt_body":"Investigate the Step 17 to Step 18b final-report flow, especially EMIT_BODY, WFR_RC, .step17-emitted, .step18-prebody, and summary-final.md handling. Check whether failures from token-report.sh and write-final-report.sh remain best-effort while still preserving user-visible final summaries and cleanup progress. Verify the prompt-side SKILL.md instructions and the new wrapper script agree about which component emits or writes sentinels. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"stall-recovery","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff expands stall-recovery state handling beyond the plan with new state-clearing and terminal-state mechanics.","prompt_body":"Review the stall-recovery-report.sh changes around ship-pr-state validation, malformed or empty state files, dangling symlinks, and new clear-stall or seed-terminal-state behavior. Check whether classification, retry accounting, terminal handling, and state clearing remain atomic and safe for stalled /implement sessions. Pay particular attention to cases where ship-pr-state.sh exists but is empty, malformed, non-regular, or partially populated. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"python-pr-link","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The Python PR body changes touch canonical Closes-line behavior, idempotency, and sanitization/redaction ordering.","prompt_body":"Inspect the Python changes for Closes #N composition, exact-match idempotency, prefix-collision handling, and whether compose_pr_body truly delegates to the canonical helper. Check for import-cycle risk, duplicated logic, and whether sanitization and redaction still operate on the final body. Verify the tests cover both compose_pr_body and tracking_issue.link_pr_closes behavior without masking implementation drift. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"bash-portability","focus_area":"code-quality","weight":1,"rationale":"New shell helpers and harness code should be checked for bash 3.2 portability, set -euo pitfalls, and quoting/containment edge cases.","prompt_body":"Audit the added and modified shell code for bash 3.2 compatibility, safe quoting, local variable scope, set -euo pipefail interactions, and reliable behavior when optional files are missing. Look for unsafe rm/cp/touch patterns, unguarded command substitutions, symlink or tmpdir containment gaps, and assumptions that only hold in the test stubs. Include both runtime scripts and their test harnesses when evaluating whether failures are realistically exercised. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"harness-wiring","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff updates Makefile, linting docs, and many harness references, which can fail through registry or shard drift.","prompt_body":"Review the Makefile, docs, and harness wiring for the newly added Step 18b and stall-recovery tests. Check that PHONY targets, shard membership, docs/linting entries, structure tests, and callsite allowlists are mutually consistent and do not create duplicate or unreachable targets. Assess whether new tests validate the real integration path rather than only confirming stub behavior. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
